{
  "summary": "Using Eurostat LFS data for 2009 and 2021, the paper computes NUTS-2 Location Quotients (LQ) of tertiary-educated workers in low-, medium- and high-skill (ISCO-based) occupations across Greece, Bulgaria, France, Germany and Denmark, with emphasis on six ERUA-related regions. Read through Geographical Political Economy, the maps are taken to show that capital and metropolitan regions in strong economies match graduates to high-skill jobs more efficiently than peripheral regions. The authors name five interconnected determinants of human-capital allocation: national economic dynamism, regional industrial specialisation, urbanisation, institutional capacity and demographics. They argue that human-capital mechanisms reproduce uneven development and that growth-pole spillover arguments are partially refuted. Five sets of policy recommendations aligned with the EU Skills Agenda conclude the paper.",
  "issues": [
    {
      "claim": "The LQ is normalised by total employment, not by the number of highly educated workers. It therefore measures the regional density of graduates in each occupation tier, not the incidence of over-skilling (mismatch).",
      "issue_type": "V1",
      "status": "confirmed_error",
      "severity": "major",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "input_field": "page_images",
          "page": 7,
          "observation": "In formulas 1.1–1.3, the numerator is highly educated employed persons in a given skill level divided by 'employed population in region', and the denominator is the same ratio for all countries under study. There is no conditioning on the size of the graduate workforce."
        },
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "employed population in region",
          "location_hint": "Methodological remarks, formulas 1.1–1.3"
        }
      ],
      "reasoning": "A region with a large tertiary-educated workforce (e.g., Île de France, Hovedstaden) will show high LQs in every tier at once. This mechanically produces the reported 'paradox' that these regions concentrate graduates even in low-skill jobs. Mismatch would need the share of graduates in each occupation tier, i.e., graduates in tier k divided by all employed graduates. As specified, the index cannot support the conclusions that metropolitan regions 'match' more efficiently, that over-skilling is concentrated in particular places, or that peripheral regions suffer persistent mismatch. The transnational benchmark is also dominated by Germany and France, so national education composition drives the LQs."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The results text contradicts itself and the figures it cites. This is especially clear for highly educated workers in low-skill occupations.",
      "issue_type": "V3",
      "status": "confirmed_error",
      "severity": "major",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "Hovedstaden (LQ=3.30) and Midtjyllan (LQ=3.25)",
          "location_hint": "Low-skill section, 2021"
        },
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "be found in Hovedstaden (LQ=0.82)",
          "location_hint": "Low-skill section, second 2021 paragraph"
        },
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "The German regions have low or close to the aggregate",
          "location_hint": "Low-skill section, 2021"
        },
        {
          "input_field": "page_images",
          "page": 11,
          "observation": "Figure 1.1b (2021, low-skill) shows almost all of Germany and all of Bulgaria as 'No data'. The legend's lowest class is 1.50–2.00, so no mapped region is near or below average. This contradicts the text's claims about German regions, Freiburg, and French regions 'close to the aggregate average'."
        },
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "Chemnitz, Brandenburg, Dresden, Mecklenburg-Vorpommern",
          "location_hint": "Medium-skill section: 'five' regions are listed as six, with Dresden appearing twice"
        }
      ],
      "reasoning": "Two 2021 paragraphs give incompatible LQs for Hovedstaden (3.30 vs 0.82). The second paragraph cites Figure 1.2b (medium skills), which suggests text has been misplaced between sections. Claims about German regions cannot be checked against a map that shows no data for them. These inconsistencies undermine confidence in all region-level statements, including the key claims about Île de France and Attica that the discussion builds on."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Extensive missing data and unreported LFS sample reliability at NUTS-2 for education-by-occupation cells undermine the low-skill analysis and the cross-national comparisons built on it.",
      "issue_type": "V7",
      "status": "missing_information",
      "severity": "major",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "low-skill occupations displayed many missing values (as",
          "location_hint": "Methodological remarks"
        },
        {
          "input_field": "page_images",
          "page": 10,
          "observation": "Figure 1.1a (2009) shows large grey 'No data' areas across most of France, much of Germany, Bulgaria and Greece."
        }
      ],
      "reasoning": "The paper does not report cell sizes, Eurostat reliability flags or suppression rules, or how missing cells were handled. The 'no data' regions are probably the ones whose estimates fell below publication thresholds, so the mapped regions are a selected subset. Even so, the discussion states that most peripheral regions show graduates concentrated in low-skill jobs, and it compares 2009 with 2021 as a trend. Neither claim is defensible without sample sizes, confidence intervals and a consistent set of regions."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The five 'determinants' and the refutation of growth-pole spillovers are causal claims that the analysis never tests. The analysis consists only of descriptive LQ maps for two years.",
      "issue_type": "V8",
